//! The clock key `pll240`: the CPU on the BBPLL at 240 MHz, the PLL at 480
//! MHz divided by 2, with both LDOs raised first to the settings ESP-IDF
//! runs 240 MHz at.
//!
//! One of the `s3clock` modules, mapped by a case's `clock` (the runner) or an
//! image's `[keys]` (`sosimage`). The HAL re-exports its `clock_init`, which
//! `kmain` calls first, before anything prints or is timed and before the
//! loader (`../../kernel/lib.saw`). `../../ABI.md` §14 has the sequence
//! ESP-IDF v5.4.1 runs, the source of each step, and which of it this takes.
//!
//! The sequence is ESP-IDF's `rtc_clk_cpu_freq_set_config` for 240 MHz
//! (esp_hw_support/port/esp32s3/rtc_clk.c), in order (`s3clockkit`'s
//! `pll_ldo_switch`):
//!
//!   * the LDO settings: ESP-IDF's `g_rtc_dbias_pvt_240m` and
//!     `g_dig_dbias_pvt_240m`, computed from the eFuse's calibration
//!     (`s3dbias`, `../../dbias/`) and held to the range ESP-IDF's
//!     arithmetic gives them. Out of it, a line says so, nothing is written,
//!     and the boot goes on at the clock the ROM left;
//!   * the BBPLL powered and set to 480 MHz, as `pll80` does
//!     (`rtc_clk_bbpll_enable`, `rtc_clk_bbpll_configure`);
//!   * `rtc_clk_cpu_freq_to_pll_mhz(240)` going up: I2C_DIG_REG (block 0x6D,
//!     host 1; soc/esp32s3/include/soc/regi2c_dig_reg.h) register 4,
//!     EXT_RTC_DREG [4:0], to the RTC setting, then register 6, EXT_DIG_DREG
//!     [4:0], to the digital one, then 40 us for the voltage to settle;
//!   * its three stores: CPUPERIOD_SEL 2, the pre-divider to 1, the source
//!     to the PLL.
//!
//! `rtc_init`'s two writes of the `non_240m` settings, which an application
//! makes at startup, are not made: the two writes above replace the same two
//! fields before the clock moves. On a CPU found on the PLL already the
//! bring-up is not run again, and the two writes and the wait are made
//! whatever speed it was found at; at 240 they write the settings ESP-IDF
//! holds there, where ESP-IDF, not going up, writes nothing.
//!
//! What moves with it, beside `pll80`'s list: APB stays 80 MHz on the PLL;
//! the memory-SPI core clock follows CORE_CLK_SEL and the PLL, not
//! CPUPERIOD_SEL, so the flash stays 20 MHz. The LDO slaves stay as the ROM
//! leaves them, all six on, which is what ESP-IDF opens at 240 MHz.
//!
//! Every word written is volatile, the LDO settings with the rest: a power
//! cycle puts each back. The eFuse is only read. No key lowers the clock from
//! 240 MHz: each below it stops on a CPU found there (`write_not_lowered`).
//!
//! It reaches every register through `s3clockkit`'s views and the ROM's
//! functions rather than the HAL's drivers: the HAL names nothing here, so an
//! image built at the `rom` key is the bytes it would be without the keys.

import s3clockkit.{CPUPERIOD_240M, ClockWords, clock_words, efuse_dbias,
                   pll_ldo_switch}
import s3dbias.{Dbias}

/// Set the CPU clock this image is built for: the PLL at 240 MHz, with the
/// LDOs raised from the eFuse's settings first.
public func clock_init() unsafe {
    clock_init_found(clock_words())
}

/// `clock_init` on a CPU whose clock words read `found`.
public func clock_init_found(found: ClockWords) unsafe {
    clock_init_at(efuse_dbias(), found)
}

/// `clock_init` with the LDO settings `dbias`, on a CPU whose clock words
/// read `found`. Settings outside the range ESP-IDF's arithmetic gives them
/// stop it before anything is written.
public func clock_init_at(dbias: Dbias, found: ClockWords) unsafe {
    pll_ldo_switch("pll240", dbias, rtc: dbias.rtc_240m, dig: dbias.dig_240m,
                   period: CPUPERIOD_240M, found: found)
}
